nsion to iterate through the scale factors in `ss`.",\n        "Interpolate the tensor `x` using `nn.functional.interpolate` with the current scale factor and mode \'nearest\'.",\n        "Ensure that the images are loaded as tensors and normalized properly before interpolation.",\n        "Using `transforms.ToTensor()` converts PIL images to tensors, and normalizing the tensor ensures that the pixel values are within the range [-1, 1].",\n        "You can also use `PIL.Image.resize` for resizing images, which might offer better control over the resizing process:",\n        "Define a function `resize_image` to resize an image based on a given scale factor:",\n        "Get the original width and height of the image.",\n        "Calculate the new width and height by multiplying the original dimensions by the scale factor.",\n        "Resize the image using `img.resize` with the new dimensions and nearest neighbor interpolation.",\n        "Define another function `load_and_resize_images` to load and resize the images:",\n        "Iterate through the list of PNG files in the \'pokemon\' directory.",\n        "Open each image using `Image.open`.",\n        "Resize the image using the `resize_image` function with a scale factor of 1/2.",\n        "Convert the resized image to a tensor using `transforms.ToTensor()`.",\n        "Append the converted tensor to the `pokemon` list.",\n        "Convert the list of tensors to a stack using `torch.stack`.",\n        "Normalize the tensor by subtracting 0.5 and dividing by 0.5 to get values in the range [-1, 1].",\n        "Return the preprocessed tensor.",\n        "Where to read more:",\n        "For more information on tensor operations, check out the [PyTorch documentation on tensors](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/tensors.html).",\n        "For details on image transformations, refer to the [PyTorch documentation on torchvision.transforms](https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/transforms.html).",\n        "For advanced tensor m