t introduce non-differentiable points, causing NaN gradients.\\n\\n2) To address these issues, consider using PyTorch\'s built-in `binary_cross_entropy_with_logits` function, which handles numerical stability better. You can also simplify your loss calculation by removing the unnecessary terms:\\n\\n```python\\nimport torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\nclass CustomLoss(torch.nn.Module):\\n    def __init__(self, pos_weight=1):\\n        super().__init__()\\n        self.pos_weight = pos_weight\\n\\n    def forward(self, input, target):\\n        # Ensure target is binary (0 or 1)\\n        target_binary = (target == 0.5).float()  # Convert 0.5 to 0 or 1\\n        bce_loss = F.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits(input, target_binary, pos_weight=self.pos_weight)\\n        return bce_loss\\n```\\n\\nThis approach ensures that the target is correctly handled as binary, avoiding potential issues with non-differentiable points and improving numerical stability.\\n\\n3) Where to read more:\\n- [torch.nn.functional.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits][§1]"\n}\nOutput: ', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct', 'n': 3, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.3}}
